cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this rule, its proof, or this implementation, please cite it using
  the metadata below.
title: "Grand Unified Divisibility Rule"
abstract: >-
  A division-free, fully traceable implementation of the general divisibility
  criterion: a number is divisible by d exactly when the weighted sum of its
  digits is divisible by d, where the weight of the ones digit is 1 and every
  subsequent weight is the previous one multiplied by 10 and then reduced below
  d by repeated subtraction; the final total is the exact remainder. The
  classical rules for 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9 and 11 are rows of the resulting table.
  This criterion is NOT new: it is Pascal's divisibility criterion, described in
  De numeris multiplicibus around 1654, and it is presented here as an
  independent rediscovery rather than a contribution. The original contributions
  of this work are narrow and stated as such: a closed form for the coefficients
  of the associated trimming test read off the divisor's last digit alone
  (x = 10 + b2 - b2*CEIL(10/b2), y = CEIL(10/b2) - 1); the exactness criterion
  gcd((y*a2)+1, d) = 1 for that particular parametrization together with the
  complete list of divisors below 100 where it fails; the implementation, its
  step-by-step trace, exhaustive verification over all 10,000 dividend/divisor
  pairs from 1 to 100, and a four-language interactive visualization. The
  trimming test itself belongs to the family published by Zbikowski in 1861 and
  is one member of the two-parameter family of Grinberg and Luryi (2014); the
  equivalence of trimming and summing tests is due to O'Shea (2019). See the
  Prior Art section of the README for a claim-by-claim mapping to the
  literature.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Ergin
    given-names: Lemi Orhan
    website: "https://www.lemiorhanergin.com"
